<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Balikh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Balikh River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Balikh is a river of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> that rises near the Turkish–Syrian border and flows south for about 100 kilometres to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqa">Raqqa</a>. Its valley was a corridor for trade and armies, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> near its upper course and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> city of Tuttul, at Tell Bi'a, at its mouth. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it formed part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyar-mudar">Diyar Mudar</a>, the western province of the Jazira.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balikh-river.jpg" alt="Balikh" width="320" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: X. Guimard · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bassin_Tigre_Euphrate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
